# Pulp API usage

Before wiring an endpoint, read its operation in the OpenAPI spec (`client/openapi/pulp.json`)
or the generated client: its params, filters, `ordering` enum and declared responses.

## Request URLs

Build every request URL with the helpers in `queries/utils/pulpApi.ts` and
`queries/utils/pulpRequest.ts`, which `npm run generate` types against the spec
(`client/src/app/client/pulpEndpoints.gen.ts`); never write a path as a string literal or
template (appending an id or a query string to a helper's result is fine). A typo, a missing
trailing slash or an action the resource doesn't have fails to compile.

- A fixed endpoint: `pulpGet({ endpoint: "tasks/", domain, params })` /
  `pulpPost({ endpoint, domain, body })`, which also type the params, body and response from
  the spec. Other methods, or a body the spec can't check (a field-driven form's
  `Record<string, unknown>`): `pulpApiPath(endpoint, domain)` with the generated response type.
- A plugin type's collection, from the catalog's `apiPath`:
  `pulpCollectionPath({ collection: "remotes", apiPath, domain, action? })`.
- An action or sub-resource of an object: `hrefAction({ kind?, href, action })`, with `kind`
  when the resource kind is known (`"repositories"`); the object itself: `toProxyHref(href)`.
- Outside any domain (`status/`, `livez/`): `pulpRootApiPath(endpoint)`.
- Never write the domain segment: the server may run with or without `DOMAIN_ENABLED`, and
  `pulpApiPath` adds it from the `IPulpDomain`.
- `pulpApiPath(endpoint, domain)` and `pulpHref(endpoint, domain)` stay positional like the
  `build*Href` builders: two fixed, obvious args read best as a path. The other helpers take
  one object.

## Writes and tasks

- Type a write by the responses its operation declares; don't assume a task. Read a `200 | 202`
  answer by its status (`toWriteResult`), never by looking for a `task` key in the body. A plugin
  definition declares what its endpoints answer (a content type's `upload`, an action's
  `responseKind`), checked against the spec by `plugins/specConformance.test.ts`. A `202`'s new
  object is `created_resources[0]` on the finished task.
- Task completion uses `isTerminalTaskState` (`queries/tasks.ts`), which includes `skipped`;
  never `state === "completed"` alone.
- Cancel is `PATCH {task_href}` `{ state: "canceled" }`; `DELETE` removes the task record and
  is not a cancel.
- Show a failed task's `error.description` as returned; don't rewrite it.

## Errors

- Turn API errors into UI text with `getMutationErrorMessage({ error, fallback })`; don't parse
  `error.response.data` ad hoc. Error body types live in `queries/utils/openapiErrorTypes.ts`.
- RBAC filters lists and details, so a `404` can mean "no permission": classify with
  `isNotFoundError` / `isForbiddenError` (`utils/isHttpError.ts`).

## Lists and filters

- Sortable columns come only from the endpoint's `ordering` enum (`OrderingField`); a filter
  must exist in the operation's query params, or Pulp answers `400 Invalid Filter`.
- `q` expressions quote their values and stay within the complexity cap of 8.
- Batch related lookups per page with `pulp_href__in` (`id__in` for users and groups) and guard
  an empty list, which Pulp ignores.

## Permissions

- Object actions read `my_permissions/` through `useObjectPermissions({ href })`.
- `my_permissions/` returns object-level roles only — not superuser status, nor global or
  domain roles. `useObjectPermissions` adds a superuser check (reading a user record 403s for
  a non-superuser) so a superuser has every action; a non-superuser's global role still can't
  be seen (no endpoint exposes it).
- There is no "can I create?" endpoint: create buttons stay enabled and a `403` shows as the
  form's submission error.

## Clearing an optional field

An edit `PATCH` that omits a field leaves it unchanged, so to clear one, send its empty value,
and match the field's type: a text field clears on `""` (`email`, `first_name`), a nullable
field only on `null` (a role's `description` rejects `""`). Don't coerce empty to `undefined`
on edit, or the old value sticks.

## Deprecated API

Don't build new UI on deprecated fields or endpoints (`deprecated: true` in the spec; ESLint
`no-deprecated` flags generated-client usage).

## Checklist

Pass/fail rubric for `/eval`. Check changed files under `queries/`, `hooks/` and plugin
definitions.

- [ ] Request URLs come from `pulpGet` / `pulpPost`, `pulpApiPath`, `pulpCollectionPath`,
      `hrefAction`, `pulpRootApiPath` or `toProxyHref`; no URL string or template, and no
      hand-built domain segment.
- [ ] Write responses are typed from the operation's declared statuses; task handling doesn't
      assume every write returns `202`.
- [ ] Task completion uses `isTerminalTaskState` (includes `skipped`); cancel is a `PATCH` with
      `state: "canceled"`.
- [ ] API errors become text via `getMutationErrorMessage`; no ad hoc error parsing.
- [ ] Sort keys come from the `ordering` enum; filters exist in the operation's params; `q`
      values are quoted and within the complexity cap; empty `__in` lists are guarded.
- [ ] Object permissions come from `useObjectPermissions` (which also covers superusers, who
      are absent from `my_permissions/`); creates aren't pre-checked.
- [ ] An edit clears an optional field by sending its empty value (`""` for text, `null` for a
      nullable field), not by omitting it.
- [ ] No new use of deprecated fields or endpoints.
